Valkey Search 1.3 release notes
===============================

================================================================================
Valkey Search 1.3.0 GA - Released Tue Nov 17th 2026
================================================================================

This is the general availability release of Valkey Search 1.3. It adds BM25STD
relevance scoring for full-text queries; FT.HYBRID, which fuses the rankings of
a text, tag or numeric query and a vector search over one index into a single
result list; full-text search in 11 languages besides English; vector range
queries; cursors for paging through large result sets; FLOAT16 and BFLOAT16
vector types; and new FT.AGGREGATE reducers and functions. Vectors in HASH
documents are now stored once and shared between Valkey and the search index
instead of being duplicated, and the numeric and tag indexes use less memory.
This release requires Valkey core 9.1.0 or higher. This section lists every
change since 1.2, including those in the release candidates.

Several compatibility fixes are breaking changes, so they are off by default;
set search.emulate-release to 1.3.0 to enable them. See Known Compatibility
Defect Corrections
(https://github.com/valkey-io/valkey-search/blob/1.3/COMPATIBILITY.md#known-compatibility-defect-corrections).
The search_compatibility_usage INFO field reports how often each legacy behavior
has been used; it replaces the search_compatibility-* INFO fields of the release
candidates.

Upgrade notes
=============
* The minimum Valkey core version is now 9.1.0 (1.2 required 9.0.1)
* Non-vector FT.SEARCH results without SORTBY are now ordered by relevance score, with ties in key order, and a KNN query filtered by text or tag clauses ranks its K nearest documents by that score instead of by distance. Add SORTBY for a fixed order (#1287, #1490)
* 1.2.x cannot load an RDB file that contains an index using FLOAT16 or BFLOAT16 vectors, FILTER, or a LANGUAGE other than English. In a cluster, upgrade every node before creating such indexes or using other features new in 1.3
* The search_vector_externing_* INFO fields are removed; vector sharing is reported by the search_vector_registry_* fields (#1316)
* FT._DEBUG no longer requires debug mode; it is in the @search, @slow, @admin and @dangerous ACL categories, so a user granted @search can run it. Restrict it with ACLs where needed (#914)

New Features
============
Major features:
* BM25STD text scoring: full-text results are ranked by relevance with BM25STD, including prefix, suffix, fuzzy, tag prefix and stemmed terms. Adds WITHSCORES, the SCORER option and the search.default-scorer config, $weight query attributes, ADDSCORES on FT.AGGREGATE, and per-document scores through the new SCORE_FIELD option on FT.CREATE; SCORE now accepts any value from 0.0 to 1.0 (#1287, #1350, #1354, #1345, #1400, #1008)
* FT.HYBRID score fusion: the new FT.HYBRID command runs two searches over one index, a SEARCH arm (a text, tag or numeric query, scored for relevance as in FT.SEARCH) and a VSIM arm (a vector similarity search, scored by distance), ranks each independently, and fuses the two ranked lists into one with RRF, LINEAR or a custom FUNCTION. Unlike a filtered vector query in FT.SEARCH, where the text, tag or numeric clauses only narrow the KNN results, both arms contribute to the final ranking: documents found by both are ranked higher, and the COMBINE clause sets how the two arms' scores are weighed against each other. The fused results pass through the FT.AGGREGATE processing stages (#1083)
* Shared vector memory: vectors in HASH documents are stored once and shared between Valkey and every index that uses them, instead of being duplicated in the search index, and RDB load reads vectors from the keyspace. HNSW ingestion and search throughput is on par with 1.2, and FLAT COSINE ingestion is faster. Enabled by default and controlled by the --enable-vector-sharing module argument; reported by the search_vector_registry_* INFO fields; search.vector-unshare-batch-size sets how many vectors are copied back to the keyspace per cron tick after FT.DROPINDEX (#1168, #1232, #1316, #1325, #1487)
* Search and aggregation cursors: WITHCURSOR [COUNT <count>] [MAXIDLE <ms>] on FT.SEARCH, FT.AGGREGATE and FT.HYBRID, and the new FT.CURSOR READ and FT.CURSOR DEL commands, for paging through large result sets. Limits are set by search.cursor-max-count and search.cursor-max-idle-ms, and open cursors are reported by search_num_cursors (#1394)
* FLOAT16 and BFLOAT16 vectors: new vector element types for FLAT and HNSW indexes, across all distance metrics, for HASH and JSON documents, roughly halving the memory used per vector (#1001)
* Multi-language full-text search: TEXT fields can be indexed in 12 Snowball stemmer languages, chosen per index with the LANGUAGE option on FT.CREATE: English, French, German, Spanish, Italian, Portuguese, Russian, Swedish, Turkish, Dutch, Indonesian and Arabic. Each language has its own tokenization, normalization, stop words and stemming (#1263)
* Vector range queries: @field:[VECTOR_RANGE <radius> $blob] in FT.SEARCH and FT.AGGREGATE queries returns the documents whose vector is within <radius>, optionally with each distance through $YIELD_DISTANCE_AS, and can be combined with other query clauses using AND, OR and negation. On HNSW fields the result is approximate, and EF_RUNTIME and $EPSILON control how widely each query searches. Each shard keeps at most search.max-nonvector-search-results-fetched matches (default 100000), not necessarily the nearest, and silently drops the rest; the search_vector_range_results_fetched_limited_count INFO field counts, per shard, the queries that dropped matches (#985, #1546)

Other new features:
* FILTER option on FT.CREATE: a boolean expression evaluated during ingestion so that only matching keys are indexed. FT.INFO counts rejections in filter_rejected_keys (#984)
* INKEYS option on FT.SEARCH to restrict results to a given set of keys (#967)
* Filtered KNN execution strategy: a KNN query with a filter can now select how the filter and the vector search are combined, instead of leaving it to the planner. The HYBRID_POLICY option only controls filtered KNN execution and is unrelated to FT.HYBRID score fusion. HYBRID_POLICY ADHOC_BF evaluates the filter first and computes exact distances for the matching documents, and HYBRID_POLICY BATCHES searches the vector index first and applies the filter inline. Both are set inside the KNN clause of FT.SEARCH and FT.AGGREGATE queries; FT.HYBRID takes POLICY ADHOC or POLICY BATCHES inside a counted VSIM FILTER, where it applies to the VSIM arm's filter (#1460)
* FT.AGGREGATE enhancements: the TOLIST, FIRST_VALUE, RANDOM_SAMPLE, COUNT_DISTINCTISH and QUANTILE reducers (COUNT_DISTINCTISH and QUANTILE are approximate); the case() function; array values in expressions, with element-wise arithmetic; GROUPBY over multi-value fields, bounded by search.max-group-key-expansion; and JSON paths and AS aliases in LOAD (AS requires search.emulate-release 1.3.0) (#932, #946, #949, #893, #1271, #1084, #1719, #1721, #1433)
* More query and index syntax is accepted: field-scoped groups such as @title:(hello | world), indexing one field under two aliases (for example as both TEXT and TAG), the NOHL and SORTABLE UNF options on FT.CREATE, with SORTABLE and UNF reported for each attribute by FT.INFO, and SORTBY on a KNN score alias in ascending order (#1218, #1196, #1395, #1267)
* In cluster mode, FT.SEARCH and FT.AGGREGATE can run inside MULTI/EXEC or Lua scripts when the index is single-slot (hash-tagged) and the local node owns that slot (#866)
* Compatibility framework: the new search.emulate-release config selects which release's behavior to emulate, so compatibility fixes can ship in minor and patch releases without changing default behavior. The search_compatibility_usage INFO field reports how often each legacy behavior has been used, and COMPATIBILITY.md documents the compatibility contract (#1063, #981, #1059, #1386, #1729)
* Resource protection: queries are rejected before fan-out when the reader queue exceeds search.max-query-queue-depth (default 100000, 0 disables the check), and queries that are already cancelled are dropped before they run; search.max-timeout-ms caps the TIMEOUT a query may request (default and maximum 60000); and queued mutations reserve memory in proportion to their expected index size, so maxmemory checks account for pending ingestion (#1276, #1368, #934)
* search.debug-mode can now be changed at runtime with CONFIG SET, so developer configs can be adjusted on a running server; developer configs in the config file are accepted at load regardless of debug mode (#1485)
* The cluster coordinator supports IPv6 node addresses (#759)
* The search_coordinator_server_search_index_partition_cpu_time_usec INFO field reports the CPU time a node spends handling queries fanned out from other nodes (#923)

Optimizations
=============
* Reimplemented the numeric index as an order-statistic B+ tree, using up to 2.4x less memory for high-cardinality fields, with faster inserts, deletes and range counts (#1030)
* Reimplemented the tag index on a radix (rax) tree, substantially reducing tag index memory (#1031)
* FT.AGGREGATE reducers process each group's records as a batch; COUNT, for example, no longer increments once per record (#816)
* FT.AGGREGATE queries that return no document content skip an unnecessary revalidation step on the main thread (#1400)
* Filtering and scoring share a per-query cache of resolved text terms and tag values, so each is looked up once per query instead of once per candidate document. The main thread no longer waits on the index-wide lock to revalidate results. In benchmarks, queries that combine NUMERIC and TAG filters have more than twice the throughput, TEXT queries filtered by TAG or NUMERIC clauses have 17% to 68% more, and KNN queries with a TAG or NUMERIC prefilter have 8% to 16% more; with concurrent writes, read p95 latency drops by up to 70%. Pure TEXT queries are unchanged (#1472)
* Large internal hash tables now grow incrementally instead of rehashing all at once, removing main-thread stalls of up to about 2 seconds during bulk ingestion (#1622)
* With Valkey core 9.2 or higher, vector index memory and large internal hash tables are allocated in 2 MB transparent huge pages unless those are disabled for the process or host; search.vector-chunk-huge-pages (default yes) controls this for vector index memory. Vector memory is then counted in 2 MB units, so after most keys of a FLAT index are deleted, used_memory stays near its peak until new vectors reuse the space (#1622, #1647)

Bug fixes
=========
* Fixed server crashes and hangs in FT.AGGREGATE expressions: APPLY of a string function (lower, upper, strlen, startswith, contains, substr, concat, timefmt, parsetime) on a missing value aborted the server, timefmt could loop forever on a format that produces no output, and lower and upper aborted the server on a value that is not valid UTF-8. A TEXT field is now also accepted as an APPLY operand (#963, #1086, #1366, #1699)
* Fixed memory corruption when an FT.AGGREGATE alias was reused for a different field (#1273)
* Fixed reads of freed memory in replies: FT.HYBRID LOAD of named fields could return missing or garbled columns, and FT.CURSOR READ could return the wrong value for a parameter bound with PARAMS in FT.AGGREGATE or FT.HYBRID (#1690, #1650)
* Fixed deadlocks and crashes around forks and thread pools: a permanent main-thread deadlock when FT.SEARCH drained the MULTI/EXEC mutation queue during BGSAVE, AOF rewrite or a replica full sync; a crash when a thread pool was resized during a fork; and a lock-ordering deadlock during index destruction (#1292, #1331, #967)
* Fixed a crash on single-slot index queries when replica-only fanout is selected, and a crash in the latency sampler when histogram allocation fails under memory pressure (#1226, #1230)
* Fixed vector crashes and hangs: a worker thread could crash when several threads used the same distance function for the first time at once, and on aarch64 a writer thread could hang when an HNSW insert computed a NaN distance. The module is no longer built with -ffast-math, so distances can differ from 1.2 in the last digits (#1396, #1542)
* FLAT vector indexes no longer restart their internal ids after an RDB load (restart, replica full sync or restore); in 1.2.1, a key added after the load overwrote a loaded key's vector, so KNN queries could return the wrong key (#985)
* Fixed HNSW issues: RDB load failing for a small index whose top level exceeds its element count; RDB load validation rejecting duplicate labels left behind by concurrent updates and deletes, which are now handled on load; insertion failures among deleted (tombstoned) nodes, including recovery from a deleted entry point; and prefetches that could read invalid neighbor ids from empty neighbor lists (#1349, #1283, #1362, #1320)
* Shared vector memory fixes: RDB load no longer aborts when an indexed key is missing from the keyspace or no longer holds a valid vector, for example after it expired; copying a vector back to a key whose vector field was deleted or expired no longer crashes the server; and renaming or moving a key after FT.DROPINDEX, before its vector has been copied back, no longer corrupts the vector (#1589, #1675, #1676)
* Keys whose name is the empty string are now indexed (#1171, #1310)
* FT.SEARCH fixes: SORTBY on an attribute renamed with AS now sorts by that attribute, SORTBY with NOCONTENT now returns sorted results in standalone and cluster mode, a bare * query runs as a match-all search instead of failing with a syntax error, PARAMS substitution now applies to the AS alias of a KNN score, and a query whose PARAMS include a parameter it does not reference is no longer rejected, matching FT.AGGREGATE (#1579, #1217, #960, #1090, #1407)
* FT.AGGREGATE fixes: fields referenced only by GROUPBY, REDUCE, SORTBY, APPLY or FILTER are now loaded automatically instead of silently evaluating as missing, except under LOAD * on JSON below search.emulate-release 1.3.0; numeric values keep full precision in GROUPBY keys and vector query results instead of being rounded to 6 significant digits; GROUPBY 0 is now accepted, reducing all records into a single group; VERBATIM, INORDER and SLOP are now applied instead of ignored; and the distance field of a text=>[KNN ...] or tag=>[KNN ...] query holds the KNN distance, not the relevance score (#1341, #1264, #1464, #1606, #1494)
* Numeric fields now treat every spelling of NaN, such as -nan or nan(1), as invalid data; previously such a value could not be removed from the index when its key was deleted. SORTBY in FT.SEARCH and FT.AGGREGATE places NaN values after every number (#1554)
* In FT.SEARCH, a KNN query filtered by tag clauses, such as @cat:{a}=>[KNN 3 @vec $q], now scores the tag clauses and ranks the K nearest documents by that score, as text-filtered KNN queries do; documents with equal scores are returned in key order. Previously results came back in distance order. To keep distance order, add SORTBY on the distance field (__<field>_score, or the name given with AS) (#1490)
* A $weight of 0 is now accepted, so a clause can restrict results without changing their scores; negative weights are still rejected. A document whose relevance sums to 0 now scores 0 even when its SCORE_FIELD value is inf or -inf (#1505)
* FT.SEARCH and FT.AGGREGATE text queries that run on the main thread (inside MULTI/EXEC or a Lua script) now wait for in-flight indexing of their result keys before revalidating them, bounded by the query's TIMEOUT, and return the timeout error past that. Previously they could return a document that no longer matched, or drop one that did (#1610)
* Tag queries on an index with text fields now wait for in-flight indexing of their result keys before rescoring them, so scores reflect the latest text (#1491)
* KNN queries whose filter is applied inline during the HNSW search, as with HYBRID_POLICY BATCHES, now check text terms joined by AND; previously documents that did not match them could be returned (#1680)
* Fixed stemming races with concurrent writes: BM25STD scores of stemmed terms could be slightly too high when concurrent deletes undercounted a stem's document frequency, and a word deleted from one key while another key added it could be dropped from its stem's word list (#1723, #1436)
* Text queries checked one document at a time, such as a stemmed word combined with numeric or tag clauses, now match documents that contain only another form of the word; previously only the word as written or its stem root matched (#1472)
* Text tokenization fixes: a multi-byte character in PUNCTUATION, such as the em dash (—), is now matched as a whole character (matching byte by byte also split words at unrelated characters sharing those bytes); and in English indexes, non-ASCII words are Unicode-normalized (NFC) before case folding, so composed and decomposed forms of the same accented letter match (#1263)
* FT.CREATE now keeps LANGUAGE when another option, such as PREFIX or SCORE, follows it; previously the index silently used English (#1702)
* FLUSHALL now drops the indexes of every database. Previously only the indexes of database 0 were dropped, so indexes in other databases kept returning the flushed keys and could not be recreated under the same name (#1404)
* Cluster fixes: query results exclude keys in slots the node does not own, avoiding double counting during slot migration; HNSW KNN queries that omit EF_RUNTIME now use the index's EF_RUNTIME on remote shards too, instead of an EF of K that could lower recall; indexes in a non-zero database no longer fail with a slot fingerprint mismatch after an RDB load; and a node demoted to replica while fetching index metadata no longer applies it (#921, #1460, #1259, #1304)
* In cluster mode, a node no longer rejects index metadata from other nodes as requiring a newer module version once the metadata revision, which grows with every index creation and deletion, exceeds the module's packed version number. The check now compares the metadata's minimum required module version (#1447)
* FT.HYBRID fixes: a match-all (*) SEARCH arm now rescores documents updated while the query runs; with LOAD * on a JSON index, later stages can read the document's fields; and in cluster mode, remote shards are reached when nodes announce IPv6 addresses, and when one arm fails on the coordinator's own shard, that shard is dropped from both arms, as remote shards already were, instead of fusing results scored by only one arm (with search.enable-partial-results set to no, the query returns an error) (#1634, #1381, #1621, #1529)
* Fixed an abort during RDB save when a key queued by MULTI/EXEC no longer had a pending mutation; such keys are now skipped (#1361)
* Writes still queued from MULTI/EXEC or a Lua script are now indexed before the server forks for BGSAVE, AOF rewrite or a replica full sync, instead of being saved for the loading node to index (#1659)
* The search_number_of_indexes INFO field is now correct: a stale RDB restore counter no longer inflates it, and replicas report the right count after a full sync (#1312, #1265)
* Fixed a memory leak on every RDB load, and index schemas are now freed asynchronously even if recreating them fails after FLUSHDB (#941, #1204)
* Reworked the module's memory allocation so that the module and the libraries statically linked into it allocate through Valkey's allocator, removing a class of allocator-mismatch heap corruption; libstdc++ is now linked statically (#1360)
* Fixed the Alpine (musl) build and musl-only crashes, including a crash in INFO and crashes during shutdown (#1402)
* Fixed the native build on Amazon Linux 2 (#1574)
* A startup log message emitted at WARNING for every INFO field is now logged at DEBUG (#1281)
* --debug-mode is now applied before the other module arguments, so developer configs passed as module arguments load in any order (#1562)
* Compatibility fixes, enabled by setting search.emulate-release to 1.3.0 (#1155, #971, #1084, #1086, #1271, #1366, #1373, #1364, #1445, #1389, #1352, #1263, #1075, #1536, #1528, #1381, #1700):
    ** A key with a malformed NUMERIC, VECTOR or TAG value (TAG values must be valid UTF-8) is dropped from the index entirely, rather than only that field being skipped
    ** FT.AGGREGATE: reducers without AS get default names; LOAD honors AS; output columns of JSON indexes are named as written in the query; division by zero, the precedence of ^ and &&, empty strings, SUM, AVG, MIN and MAX over empty or non-numeric input, and references to missing fields follow the corrected behavior; and a LIMIT next to SORTBY raises the SORTBY bound, so paging past the first 10 sorted rows works
    ** FT.AGGREGATE expressions are typed as in Redis 8: a comparison compares numbers when either operand is a number, and otherwise strings when either is a string, and <, <=, > or >= between a number and a non-numeric string is an error; && and || evaluate their right operand only when needed; strlen, startswith, contains and substr reject numbers; and a PARAMS value that reads as a number is a number. FT.CREATE FILTER and FT.HYBRID always follow these rules. In FT.AGGREGATE on HASH indexes, a NUMERIC field fetched only by LOAD * keeps its stored string value
    ** FT.AGGREGATE with LOAD * also fetches the fields named in other LOAD clauses and the fields that later stages read, so on JSON indexes those stages see the fields. FT.HYBRID always does this
    ** FT.SEARCH: an unescaped ' delimits a phrase; WITHSORTKEYS prefixes string sort keys with $ and returns nil for documents without a sort key; and only the last RETURN clause applies
    ** Expression functions: non-empty strings are truthy; lower and upper return nil for non-string arguments; numeric functions return NaN for non-numeric strings; month() rounds correctly; and parsetime, timefmt and the date functions return nil for invalid input
    ** FT.INFO reports the configured SCORE and SCORE_FIELD of an index
    ** A query containing malformed UTF-8 is rejected; and on English indexes, MINSTEMSIZE and fuzzy edit distance count characters (Unicode code points) instead of bytes, as other languages always do
* Improvements to tests and test infrastructure with no corresponding module code changes.

